  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on the skewness of the lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more left-skewed (i.e., where a greater portion of the probability is assigned to favorable outcomes). To assess skewness, we examine how the probabilities are distributed across favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. In both lotteries, the favorable outcome is the positive payout (4000 or 3000 Euros), and the unfavorable outcome is 0 Euros. Lottery A has a 20% chance of 4000 and 80% chance of 0; Lottery B has a 25% chance of 3000 and 75% chance of 0. Thus, Lottery B assigns a higher probability (25%) to its favorable outcome than Lottery A (20%). However, the magnitude of the favorable outcome is higher in A. Skewness would depend on both the direction and magnitude of deviations from the mean, but the reason simplifies this to 'portion of probability on favorable outcomes'. Since B has a higher probability of a positive payout, it is relatively more left-skewed than A under this interpretation. Therefore, the decision reason would prefer Lottery B. The information needed to apply this reason is available from the problem.",
