  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the skewness of the lotteries by focusing on how much probability mass is assigned to favorable outcomes. The individual, however, focuses solely on expected monetary value, calculating the average return of each option. The information used by the individual (outcomes and probabilities to compute expectation) overlaps partially with the data needed for skewness, but the transformation and purpose differ. The individual does not assess or refer to the distributional shape or probability weighting. Moreover, the decision rule applied by the individual (higher expected value) contradicts the one implied by the reason, which would favor Lottery B due to its higher win probability (and thus greater left-skew under the given definition). There is no indication that the individual considered the skewness of the outcomes or the concentration of probability on favorable results. Therefore, it is unlikely that the decision reason was used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
