{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed, meaning a greater portion of the probability is assigned to more favorable outcomes (i.e., less negative or zero outcomes in this context). To assess skewness, we need to compare the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Both lotteries involve only losses or zero outcomes, so the 'favorable' outcomes are the zero outcomes. Lottery A has a 99.9% chance of 0 Euros and a 0.1% chance of -6000 Euros. Lottery B has a 99.8% chance of 0 Euros and a 0.2% chance of -3000 Euros. The probability mass on the zero (favorable) outcome is higher in Lottery A (99.9%) than in Lottery B (99.8%). Therefore, Lottery A assigns more probability to the favorable outcome and is thus more left-skewed. The decision reason would prefer Lottery A.",
