{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed (i.e., has a higher concentration of probability on favorable outcomes). To assess skewness, we need to compare the distribution of outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A has two outcomes: 1000 EUR (50%) and 0 EUR (50%). Its distribution is symmetric around the mean (500 EUR), so it is not skewed. Lottery B has a single outcome: 500 EUR with 100% probability, which is a degenerate distribution. A degenerate distribution has no variance and thus no skewness in the traditional sense, but it can be considered perfectly symmetric. Since neither lottery is left-skewed, and Lottery A is symmetric while B is deterministic, neither clearly dominates in left-skewness. The reason would likely be indifferent between the two, or possibly favor B slightly due to certainty (though this is not strictly skewness-based). Thus, the reason can be applied, but it does not clearly prefer one lottery over the other based on skewness alone.",
